{
  "Study.Name": "Temporal Dynamics of Recovery: A 30-Day Intensive Longitudinal Study",
  "authors": "Sarah J. Martinez, PhD, Robert K. Chen, MD, Angela L. Thompson, PhD",
  "funding": "National Institute on Drug Abuse (NIDA) Grant R01DA045678; Substance Abuse and Mental Health Services Administration (SAMHSA) Award TI234567",
  "abstract": "This study examines the dynamic, day-to-day processes of recovery from substance use disorders using ecological momentary assessment (EMA). Participants (N=150) in early recovery (0-6 months abstinent) completed brief surveys twice daily (morning and evening) for 30 days. The study investigates temporal patterns of craving, mood variability, stress reactivity, and substance use lapses. Key outcomes include real-time prediction models of lapse risk and identification of high-risk temporal windows. Data were collected via smartphone-based EMA with geolocation tagging.",
